    "title": "Article 10: Continuance of the Rights of Citizenship",
    "definition": "Every person who is or is deemed to be a citizen of India under any of the foregoing provisions of this Part shall, subject to the provisions of any law that may be made by Parliament, continue to be such citizen.",
    "explanation": "Article 10 serves as a continuity provision, ensuring that citizenship acquired or deemed under Articles 5 through 9 of the Constitution does not lapse automatically but continues until Parliament legislates otherwise. This article provides constitutional stability to citizenship status while simultaneously recognizing Parliament's sovereign authority to regulate citizenship through ordinary legislation.\n\nThe article operates on two levels. First, it guarantees continuity — a person who became a citizen under the constitutional provisions of Part II does not need to periodically renew or reaffirm their citizenship. Their status persists indefinitely. Second, it subordinates this continuity to parliamentary legislation — the phrase \"subject to the provisions of any law that may be made by Parliament\" empowers Parliament to enact laws that may modify, restrict, or provide additional conditions for the continuance of citizenship.\n\nThis dual nature of Article 10 reflects the constitutional balance between individual rights and legislative sovereignty. While citizenship is a foundational status that should not be arbitrarily revoked, the Constitution also recognizes that Parliament must have the flexibility to adapt citizenship laws to changing circumstances. The Citizenship Act, 1955, enacted under Article 11 (read with Article 10), provides comprehensive rules governing not just the acquisition but also the termination and deprivation of citizenship.\n\nArticle 10 is particularly significant because it bridges the transitional provisions of Articles 5-9 with the ongoing legislative framework under Article 11. The constitutional articles (5-9) dealt primarily with the initial determination of citizenship at the commencement of the Constitution — a one-time exercise. Article 10 ensures that the citizenship so determined has continuing legal effect, while Article 11 empowers Parliament to create a permanent, evolving legislative framework.\n\nThe phrase \"subject to the provisions of any law that may be made by Parliament\" has been interpreted to mean that Parliament can prescribe conditions under which citizenship may be lost. Under the Citizenship Act, 1955, citizenship can be lost through renunciation (Section 8), termination upon acquisition of foreign citizenship (Section 9), and deprivation by the Government of India in certain circumstances (Section 10). All of these mechanisms derive their constitutional authority from the combined operation of Articles 10 and 11.\n\nArticle 10 also ensures that changes in citizenship law are prospective, not retroactive, unless Parliament specifically legislates otherwise. A person who was a citizen under Article 5 at the commencement continues to be a citizen even if subsequent legislation introduces new criteria that they might not meet. Their citizenship is protected by Article 10's continuity guarantee, unless Parliament expressly provides for retrospective application.\n\nThe provision also has implications for the relationship between fundamental rights and citizenship. Many fundamental rights under Part III are available only to \"citizens\" (as opposed to \"persons\"). Article 10 ensures that the category of \"citizens\" remains stable and legally certain, providing a firm foundation for the enjoyment of these rights.",
