    "part": "PART II — CITIZENSHIP",
    "title": "Article 11: Parliament to Regulate the Right of Citizenship by Law",
    "definition": "Nothing in the foregoing provisions of this Part shall derogate from the power of Parliament to make any provision with respect to the acquisition and termination of citizenship and all other matters relating to citizenship.",
    "explanation": "Article 11 is the capstone provision of Part II, conferring upon Parliament plenary and comprehensive legislative authority over all matters relating to citizenship. This article ensures that the constitutional provisions of Articles 5-10 do not exhaust or limit Parliament's power to legislate on citizenship. In essence, Article 11 declares that Parliament's citizenship-making power is supreme, ongoing, and unrestricted by the specific provisions of Articles 5-10.\n\nThe language of Article 11 is deliberately broad. The phrase \"acquisition and termination of citizenship and all other matters relating to citizenship\" covers the entire spectrum of citizenship law — from the criteria for acquiring citizenship (by birth, descent, registration, or naturalization) to the grounds for losing citizenship (renunciation, termination, deprivation), and everything in between (documentation, proof, registration procedures, offenses related to citizenship fraud, etc.).\n\nThe phrase \"nothing in the foregoing provisions of this Part shall derogate from\" makes clear that Articles 5-10 are not exhaustive. They were transitional provisions designed to address the immediate citizenship questions at the Constitution's commencement. Article 11 empowers Parliament to go beyond these provisions, create entirely new categories of citizenship, modify existing criteria, and adapt the citizenship framework to evolving national needs.\n\nIt is under Article 11 that Parliament enacted the Citizenship Act, 1955, which remains the primary legislation governing Indian citizenship. This Act has been amended multiple times to respond to changing circumstances — the 1986 amendment restricted jus soli citizenship, the 2003 amendment introduced the OCI scheme, and the 2019 Citizenship Amendment Act (CAA) created a fast-track naturalization pathway for persecuted minorities from neighboring countries.\n\nHowever, Article 11's power is not entirely unlimited. The Supreme Court has held that while Parliament has wide latitude in citizenship matters, it cannot legislate in a manner that violates the basic structure of the Constitution. For instance, a citizenship law that discriminates on grounds prohibited by Article 15 (religion, race, caste, sex, place of birth) could be challenged as unconstitutional. The Citizenship Amendment Act, 2019, has been challenged on precisely these grounds — critics argue that its exclusion of Muslims from the fast-track pathway violates the secular character of the Constitution, which is part of its basic structure.\n\nArticle 11 also interacts with Article 14 (right to equality) and Article 21 (right to life and personal liberty). While citizenship itself is a matter of parliamentary legislation, the process of determining citizenship must comply with fundamental rights. The Supreme Court in multiple cases has held that a person facing deportation or denial of citizenship is entitled to procedural safeguards under Article 21.\n\nThe relationship between Article 11 and Article 246 (distribution of legislative powers) is also noteworthy. Citizenship is listed in the Union List (List I, Entry 17) of the Seventh Schedule, confirming that it is exclusively within Parliament's domain. State legislatures have no power to legislate on citizenship.\n\nThe comprehensive nature of Article 11 means that Parliament can create different classes of citizens with different rights (though this would be subject to fundamental rights scrutiny), establish different pathways to citizenship for different categories of persons, and prescribe different grounds for loss of citizenship for different groups — all within the bounds of constitutional principles.",
